WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.209
The RMF process is often described through seven steps: Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, and Monitor.

2
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:13.201
These steps help organizations manage risk across the system lifecycle.

3
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:16.972
Prepare establishes the organizational and system-level context.

4
00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:26.820
Teams identify stakeholders, missions, assumptions, common controls, risk management strategy, and the information needed to make later steps more effective.

5
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:33.356
Categorize defines the potential impact if confidentiality, integrity, or availability are affected.

6
00:00:33.356 --> 00:00:39.482
Categorization shapes the level of rigor and helps determine which baseline controls may be appropriate.

7
00:00:39.482 --> 00:00:42.555
Select identifies the controls needed for the system.

8
00:00:42.555 --> 00:00:52.573
Implement turns those selected controls into real safeguards, configurations, procedures, architecture, automation, monitoring, and documentation.

9
00:00:52.573 --> 00:01:00.146
Assess evaluates whether controls are implemented correctly, operating as intended, and producing the desired outcome.

10
00:01:00.146 --> 00:01:05.753
Authorize is the formal risk decision about whether the system may operate with the remaining risk.

11
00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:09.096
Monitor keeps the risk picture current after authorization.

12
00:01:09.096 --> 00:01:15.133
Systems change, vulnerabilities appear, users change, dependencies shift, and threats evolve.

13
00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:20.411
Continuous monitoring helps the organization keep decisions grounded in current information.

14
00:01:20.411 --> 00:01:25.041
Although the steps are often shown in sequence, RMF is not purely linear.

15
00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:35.996
Findings may cause control changes, monitoring may trigger reassessment, and new mission needs may require updates to categorization, controls, or authorization conditions.

16
00:01:35.996 --> 00:01:38.720
That feedback loop is one of RMF's strengths.

17
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:47.560
It lets organizations adjust the risk picture when new information appears instead of relying on assumptions from an earlier point in the system lifecycle.
